.\" pamsignal.8 - manual page for the pamsignal daemon
.\" Maintained as pamsignal.8.in; .TH version is filled by meson at build.
.TH PAMSIGNAL 8 "2026-05-03" "pamsignal @VERSION@" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
pamsignal \- real-time PAM authentication monitor with multi-channel alerts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pamsignal
.RB [ \-f \ |\  \-\-foreground ]
.RB [ \-c \ |\  \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B pamsignal
.RB { \-t \ |\  \-\-check\-config }
.RB [ \-c
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B pamsignal
.RB { \-T \ |\  \-\-test\-alert }
.RB [ \-c
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B pamsignal
.RB { \-V \ |\  \-\-version }
.br
.B pamsignal
.RB { \-h \ |\  \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pamsignal
is a lightweight C daemon that monitors PAM authentication events through
the systemd journal. It detects login attempts, logouts, and brute-force
patterns produced by
.BR sshd (8),
.BR sudo (8),
.BR su (1),
and
.BR login (1),
optionally dispatching alerts to Telegram, Slack, Microsoft Teams, WhatsApp,
Discord, or an arbitrary HTTPS webhook.
.PP
The daemon is designed to run under
.BR systemd (1)
as the unprivileged user
.BR pamsignal ,
which must be a member of the
.B systemd-journal
group to obtain read-only access to the journal. The shipped unit uses
.B Type=notify
with
.BR WatchdogSec=30s :
the daemon reports readiness once the journal watch is set up and pets
the watchdog on every loop iteration, so a stalled daemon is restarted.
Outbound HTTP requests are
made via
.BR curl (1)
launched in a fork+exec child so a network failure cannot crash the parent
process.
.PP
At runtime the daemon refuses to start as
.BR root ,
caps its descendant process count to bound any alert-driven fork-burst, and
sets
.BR PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS
to refuse setuid escalation in any child it spawns. The shipped systemd
unit additionally applies
.BR ProtectSystem=strict ,
.BR MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes ,
.BR NoNewPrivileges=yes ,
.BR CapabilityBoundingSet= ,
and a
.BR SystemCallFilter=
allowlist; see
.BR systemd.exec (5)
for the meaning of each directive.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-foreground
Stay in the foreground (do not daemonize). Use it under a process
supervisor such as
.BR systemd (1);
the shipped
.B pamsignal.service
unit
.RB ( Type=notify )
runs the daemon this way. In foreground mode no PID file is created.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fIPATH\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read configuration from
.I PATH
instead of the compiled-in default. The path is resolved through
.BR realpath (3)
before
.BR chdir (\(dq/\(dq)
is called; an unresolvable path causes
.B pamsignal
to refuse to start (defending against symlink swaps and permission
misconfigurations).
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-check\-config
Load and validate the configuration file, then exit without starting the
monitor. Every problem is reported on standard error with its line number;
on success a short summary (enabled alert channels, number of trusted
sources, message style) is printed. Warnings, such as an unknown key, are
printed too but do not change the exit status. Exit status is 0 if the
file is valid and 1 if it is invalid or does not exist. It refuses to run
as root and does not need the
.B systemd\-journal
group. Run it as the service user so that file ownership and permission
checks match what the daemon will see:
.B sudo \-u pamsignal pamsignal \-\-check\-config
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-test\-alert
Validate the configuration, send one test message to every configured
alert channel, wait for each delivery to finish, and exit. One result line
is printed per channel; an HTTP error status from the receiver (for example
a revoked webhook or a wrong token) counts as a failure, and the underlying
.BR curl (1)
error is shown. The test message uses the configured
.B message_style
and ignores
.B enable_notification_type
and
.BR alert_cooldown_sec .
Exit status is 0 only if every configured channel accepted the message; it
is 1 if any channel failed or none is configured. Like
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
it refuses to run as root; run it as the service user.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the daemon's version (e.g. "pamsignal @VERSION@") to standard output
and exit with status 0. Runs before any privilege check, so it works
under any user (root, dpkg/rpm post-install scripts, plain operator).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a brief usage summary to standard output and exit with status 0.
The summary covers the option set, the default config path, and a
pointer to this man page and
.BR pamsignal.conf (5).
Also runs before any privilege check.
.PP
An unrecognized option, or
.B \-c
without a
.IR PATH ,
is an error:
.B pamsignal
prints a message to standard error and exits with status 2 without
starting the monitor.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration file. Parsing happens into a temporary
.B ps_config_t
first; on success the new configuration is swapped in atomically. On parse
failure the previous configuration is preserved. The in-memory brute-force
tracking table is preserved across reloads, so existing per-source
attempt counts and alert cooldowns survive the reload; if
.B max_tracked_ips
shrinks, entries beyond the new size are dropped.
.TP
.B SIGTERM
Terminate gracefully. The journal watch loop returns, the PID file (if
any) is removed, and the daemon exits with status 0.
.TP
.B SIGINT
Same handling as
.BR SIGTERM .
Useful when running interactively in
.BR \-\-foreground
mode.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/pamsignal/pamsignal.conf
The configuration file. The compiled-in default path is set at meson
configure time from
.B sysconfdir
and may differ for non-system installs. The file holds alert-channel
credentials (Telegram bot tokens, webhook URLs, optional
.B webhook_auth_header
value, optional mTLS file paths) and brute-force tuning parameters;
it is shipped with mode
.B 0640
and group
.BR pamsignal .
When the optional mTLS keys are configured, the referenced
.IR webhook_client_cert " / " webhook_client_key " / " webhook_ca_bundle
files are validated at config load (regular file, ownership in
.RB { root , euid }, " webhook_client_key"
not group- or world-readable, opened with
.BR O_NOFOLLOW ).
See
.BR pamsignal.conf (5)
for the full key reference and validation rules.
.TP
.B /run/pamsignal/pamsignal.pid
PID file written when the daemon is launched without
.BR \-\-foreground .
Created via
.BR openat (2)
under an
.BR O_DIRECTORY \  | \  O_NOFOLLOW
directory file descriptor; a stale PID file is removed only after
.BR kill (pid,\ 0)
returns
.BR ESRCH .
The directory is supplied by the systemd unit's
.B RuntimeDirectory=pamsignal
directive.
.TP
.B /usr/lib/systemd/system/pamsignal.service
The shipped systemd service unit (vendor unit path). Admin overrides
belong in
.B /etc/systemd/system/pamsignal.service.d/*.conf
drop-ins, not on top of the vendored unit.
.SH OUTPUT
All operational logging is emitted via
.BR sd_journal_send (3)
with structured fields aligned to the Elastic Common Schema (ECS).
Useful queries:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
journalctl \-t pamsignal \-f
journalctl \-u pamsignal \-\-since today
journalctl _SYSTEMD_UNIT=pamsignal.service EVENT_ACTION=brute_force_detected
.EE
.RE
.PP
Journal events carry the ECS-aligned
.BR EVENT_* ", " USER_* ", " SOURCE_* ", " HOST_* ", " SERVICE_* ", and " PROCESS_*
fields. Session events also carry
.B PAMSIGNAL_ACTOR
(the logged-in account that opened the session, as reported by
.BR pam_unix (8);
empty when not known). The legacy
.B PAMSIGNAL_*
parallel field set retained through 0.2.x for backward compatibility was
removed in 0.3.0; update any saved
.BR journalctl
queries that still use the
.B PAMSIGNAL_EVENT=...
form to
.BR EVENT_ACTION=...
instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful clean shutdown (typically via SIGTERM/SIGINT).
.TP
.B 1
Refused to start: invoked as root, current user is not in the
.B systemd\-journal
group, the configuration file path could not be resolved, or the
configuration file failed to load. For
.BR \-\-check\-config :
the file is invalid or missing. For
.BR \-\-test\-alert :
a channel failed or none is configured.
.TP
.B 2
Unrecognized option, or
.B \-c
without a
.IR PATH .
.TP
.B non\-zero (other)
An internal subsystem failed to initialize: signal handlers, daemonize,
PID-file acquisition, or journal watch. The detailed reason is logged via
.BR sd_journal_print (3)
to the journal under syslog identifier
.BR pamsignal .
.SH SECURITY
.B pamsignal
is a security-monitoring daemon and is itself written defensively. Notable
hardening choices:
.IP \(bu 2
Runs as a dedicated unprivileged user with read-only journal access; never
opens a listening socket; the only outbound network capability is the
.BR curl (1)
fork+exec path used for alert dispatch.
.IP \(bu 2
Alert credentials are passed to the curl child through a memfd-backed
.B \-\-config
file rather than via argv, so they do not appear in
.B /proc/<pid>/cmdline
or in process accounting. The same memfd carries the optional
.B webhook_auth_header
value (Bearer token, API key, HMAC, etc.) and the optional
.B webhook_client_cert " / " webhook_client_key " / " webhook_ca_bundle
paths used for mTLS, so the curl child's
.B argv
is byte-identical regardless of which authentication mode is in use.
.IP \(bu 2
Webhook URLs are constrained to
.B https://
via curl's
.B \-\-proto =https
flag. Mutual-TLS authentication for the custom webhook channel is
optional and additive with
.BR webhook_auth_header ;
.B webhook_client_key
must not be group- or world-readable (the daemon refuses to start
otherwise), and all three TLS path values are opened with
.B O_NOFOLLOW
so symlinked targets are rejected at config load.
.IP \(bu 2
Journal entries are filtered through an
.B _EXE
allowlist (\fBsshd\fR, \fBsshd\-session\fR, \fBsudo\fR, \fBsu\fR,
\fBlogin\fR, \fBsystemd\-logind\fR under
.IR /usr/ ", " /bin/ ", " /sbin/ ", " /lib/ ", " /lib64/ " or " /opt/ );
entries with a missing
.B _EXE
field are dropped, so an unprivileged user cannot inject fake PAM events
via
.BR logger (1).
.IP \(bu 2
Per-source-IP brute-force tracking with a configurable cooldown bounds the
alert volume during a sustained attack.
.IP \(bu 2
Local privilege-escalation brute-force tracking. Failed
.BR sudo (8)
and
.BR su (1)
authentications are keyed by the local actor (\fIruser\fR from the
.B pam_unix
auth-failure message); the chat alert reports
.IR actor ,
.IR target ,
.IR attempts ,
and
.I service
when the threshold is crossed. Per-event chat alerts are suppressed for
sudo/su login failures (the journal entry is still written so
.B journalctl
history is complete); only the threshold-breach alert fires, so a
single mistyped password does not page the operator.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pamsignal.conf (5),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR systemd.exec (5),
.BR systemd.unit (5),
.BR systemd-analyze (1),
.BR journalctl (1),
.BR sd_journal_send (3),
.BR sshd_config (5),
.BR pam (8)
.PP
The repository ships
.B docs/threat-model.md
documenting what
.B pamsignal
defends against, what it deliberately does not, and the design rationale
behind the split. Read it before reporting suspected vulnerabilities;
.B SECURITY.md
in the same repository documents the private-disclosure channel.
.SH BUGS
Report non-security bugs at <https://github.com/anhtuank7c/pamsignal/issues>.
For security issues, use the private channel documented in
.B SECURITY.md
(GitHub Security Advisories preferred).
.SH AUTHOR
Tuan Nguyen <anhtuank7c@hotmail.com>
